// Slonimsky's polations (Thesaurus of Scales and Melodic Patterns): tones put in,
// before, between or after the tones of a melody. Each word prints its result.
//
// Load it:  "mus/slonimsky.mus" load
// Hear one: put its line on the stack without the . and play it, e.g.
//   \tune '[59] slonimsky/infra quarters ->


// ---- the algorithms it calls ---------------------------------------------

'melodic.slonimsky 's reqalgo

// ---- the settings: plain values in the tctx -------------------------------

4 :div T!                        // number of divisions
0 :inter T!                      // interpolation flag
0 :infra T!                      // infrapolation flag
0 :ultra T!                      // ultrapolation flag

80 :soft T!                      // the volume it starts at (mf) ...
90 :loud T!                      // ... and climbs to at the climax (fff)
1.0 :width T!                    // panning of the two groups, -width and +width (0 .. 1)
3/8 :lag T!                      // how late group B is, as a note length (a pulse is 1/16)

'[64 67 71 69 74 71 67 66 64 69 71 76 74 71 67 69]  :melody T!   // E dorian, a rising and falling line

'tune '[60 62 64 65] :=                 // the principal tones: C D E F

// the numbers of a result as notes, a quarter each: ( list -- music )
: quarters ( $p -- music ) | '[1/4] [~] $p zip $p length take ;

// ---- the three single forms: ( tones insertion -- tones' ) --------------------
// The insertion is tones you chose to lie below (infra), within (inter) or above
// (ultra) the tones; it always sounds AFTER a tone, never before one, and nothing
// follows the last tone.

/*
\tune '[59] s/infra quarters .           // '[60 59 62 59 64 59 65]
\tune '[66] s/inter quarters .           // '[60 66 62 66 64 66 65]
\tune '[67] s/ultra quarters .           // '[60 67 62 67 64 67 65]

// the insertion can be several tones
\tune '[58 59] s/infra quarters .        // '[60 58 59 62 58 59 64 58 59 65]

// ---- the general form: ( tones infra inter ultra -- tones' ) ----------------------
// after each tone but the last: the infra tones, then the inter tones, then the ultra tones

\tune '[59] '[66] '[67] s/polation2 quarters .   // '[60 59 66 67 62 59 66 67 64 59 66 67 65]

// an empty list switches that part off: only infra and ultra
\tune '[59] '[] '[67] s/polation2 quarters .     // '[60 59 67 62 59 67 64 59 67 65]

// all of them off gives the tune back
\tune '[] '[] '[] s/polation2 quarters .         // '[60 62 64 65]
*/

// ---- s: the polations of an equal division of the octaves -------------
// ( divisor infra inter ultra -- pitches ), the last three 1 (on) or 0 (off), or
// ( divisor mask -- pitches ), the mask infra 1 + inter 2 + ultra 4 (5 is infra and ultra). The divisor is the interval in semitones
// stacked from C4 until a C comes back (2 3 4 5 6 8 9 10); the primary tones are those.
// After the lower tone of each pair come, in this order, the infra tone (a semitone
// below it), the inter tone (a semitone above it) and the ultra tone (a semitone above
// the higher tone of the pair), as the three ints switch them on.

/*
6 0 0 0 s/polation quarters .   // the primary tones: C4 F#4 C5
6 0 1 0 s/polation quarters .   // inter: C4 C#4 F#4 G4 C5
6 0 0 1 s/polation quarters .   // ultra: C4 G4 F#4 C#5 C5
6 1 0 0 s/polation quarters .   // infra: C4 B3 F#4 F4 C5
6 1 1 0 s/polation quarters .   // infra and inter
6 1 0 1 s/polation quarters .   // infra and ultra
6 1 1 1 s/polation quarters .   // all three
6 7 s/polation quarters .       // the same as a mask: 1 + 2 + 4
6 5 s/polation quarters .       // infra and ultra

// other divisors: 4 (the augmented triad), 3 (the diminished seventh), 2 (the whole-tone scale)
4 1 1 1 s/polation quarters .
3 0 1 0 s/polation quarters .
2 0 1 1 s/polation quarters .

// more than an octave: 8 spans two octaves, 9 three. 5 and 10 span five and end on pitch
// 120 (C9), past the 24..119 that notes can be written in: as numbers they are fine
// (10 0 0 0 s/polation .), as notes with quarters they are not.
8 0 1 0 s/polation quarters .
9 0 1 0 s/polation quarters .
*/
